[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Prefab Systems and Variant Nesting in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unity Prefabs are powerful assets that allow you to create, configure, and store a GameObject complete with all its components and child objects. Prefab Variants and Nesting take this a step further by enabling scalable hierarchies and inheritance in your game development workflows."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Prefab Nesting"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Nesting allows you to place a Prefab inside another Prefab. This creates a parent-child relationship where complex objects can be composed of smaller, reusable modular parts. For example, a Spaceship Prefab might contain a Weapon Prefab and a Shield Prefab."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Modularity: Break down massive GameObjects into smaller, maintainable chunks."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Propagation: Changes to a child Prefab automatically update all parent Prefabs that contain it (unless overridden)."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Working with Prefab Variants"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Prefab Variant is a Prefab that inherits properties from another Prefab, known as its Base Prefab. Any modifications made to the Base Prefab will automatically flow down to its variants, while local overrides on the variant remain intact."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Right-click an existing Prefab in your Project window and select Create > Prefab Variant."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Modify properties, add components, or alter child objects. Modified values turn bold in the Inspector."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Instantiating Prefabs via Script"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can instantiate nested prefabs or variants dynamically at runtime using CSharp. Ensure you assign the Prefab reference in the Unity Inspector."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;

public class Spawner : MonoBehaviour
{
    public GameObject enemyVariantPrefab;

    void Start()
    {
        if (enemyVariantPrefab != null)
        {
            Instantiate(enemyVariantPrefab, Vector3.zero, Quaternion.identity);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Large Projects"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid deep nesting beyond 3 to 4 levels to prevent performance bottlenecks and confusing override trees."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Prefab Variants for archetype variations (e.g., Base Enemy -> Fast Enemy Variant, Tank Enemy Variant)."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always apply overrides carefully to avoid breaking the data inheritance chain from the base asset."}]
